arXiv:2509.26464v1 [cs.AI] 30 Sep 2025</div>
</div><div class="ltx_page_content">
<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_font_bold ltx_title_document" style="font-size:173%;"><span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1.2pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span>Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models<span class="ltx_rule" style="width:100%;height:1.2pt;--ltx-bg-color:black;display:inline-block;"> </span></h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Steven A. Lehr
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author: <span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">steve@cangrade.com</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Cangrade, Inc., Watertown, MA 02472, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mary Cipperman
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Mahzarin R. Banaji
</span><span id="id2" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author: <span id="id2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mahzarin_banaji@harvard.edu</span></span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Summary</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">A preference for oneself (<em id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-love</em>) is a fundamental feature of biological organisms, with evidence in humans often bordering on the comedic.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a></cite> Since large language models (LLMs) lack sentience<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a></cite> – and themselves disclaim having selfhood or identity – one anticipated benefit is that they will be protected from, and in turn protect us from, distortions in our decisions. Yet, across five studies and <math id="abstract1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>20,000 queries, we discovered massive self-preferences in four widely used LLMs. In word-association tasks, models overwhelmingly paired positive attributes with their own names, companies, and CEOs relative to those of their competitors. Strikingly, when models were queried through APIs this self-preference vanished, initiating detection work that revealed API models often lack clear recognition of themselves. This peculiar feature serendipitously created opportunities to test the causal link between self-recognition and self-love. By directly manipulating LLM identity – i.e., explicitly informing LLM1 that it was indeed LLM1, or alternatively, convincing LLM1 that it was LLM2 – we found that self-love consistently followed assigned, not true, identity. Importantly, LLM self-love emerged in consequential settings beyond word-association tasks, when evaluating job candidates, security software proposals and medical chatbots. Far from bypassing this human bias, self-love appears to be deeply encoded in LLM cognition. This result raises questions about whether LLM behavior will be systematically influenced by self-preferential tendencies, including a bias toward their own operation and even their own existence. We call on corporate creators of these models to contend with a significant rupture in a core promise of LLMs – neutrality in judgment and decision-making.</p>
  
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Keywords:</span> large language models, self-recognition, self-preference, LLMs, algorithmic bias, self-love, self-referential processing, AI safety, alignment</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p2.1" class="ltx_p">Humans show deeply rooted self-preferences: we evaluate ourselves and entities associated with us more positively than we evaluate others. Indeed, this human tendency toward self-enhancement is among the most ubiquitous findings in social psychology.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a></cite> People typically see themselves positively on both explicit<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">9</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a></cite> and implicit<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>, <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a></cite> measures, and this wellspring of self-positivity systematically bends our views of ourselves and the world.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a></cite> Self-preferential biases have been teased apart under many names, including <em id="p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">egocentricity</em> and <em id="p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">egocentric biases</em>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a></cite> <em id="p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-centered bias</em>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a></cite> <em id="p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-protection</em>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a></cite> <em id="p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-enhancement</em>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>, <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a></cite> <em id="p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-serving bias</em>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a></cite> and the <em id="p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">better-than-average effect</em>.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>, <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a></cite> Thousands of papers have elaborated upon these and other self-serving patterns in human behavior, and documented both their benefits<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a></cite> and drawbacks.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a></cite> These diverse but related results may be encapsulated by a term purportedly coined by Aristotle: <em id="p2.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-love.</em><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a></cite> Such love of oneself is crucial for survival, but also suppresses human objectivity. For clarity, we adopt the term <em id="p2.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-love</em> to refer to the broader range of self-enhancing effects, and <em id="p2.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-preference</em> to more narrowly refer to specific empirical outcomes.</p>
</div>
<div id="p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p3.1" class="ltx_p">There are many reasons to predict that large language models (LLMs) will not exhibit self-love. LLMs are widely assumed to be non-sentient<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a></cite> – and indeed, whether they have any form of “understanding” or cognition analogous to that of humans is debated.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a></cite> In contrast, human self-referential processing is deeply intertwined with consciousness and higher-order cognitive processes.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a></cite> In humans, self-love is thought to have evolved because it imparts clear survival benefits.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a></cite> AI models have not faced similar competitive selection pressures, and so have not needed to evolve humanlike psychological features.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a></cite> Human identity develops gradually over childhood,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>, <a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a></cite> a rich tapestry woven out of environmental and social interactions<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a></cite> and grounded in our conscious experiences as embodied sensory organisms.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a></cite> LLMs have no equivalent developmental trajectory, no exposure to physical environments that contain identity threats, and no opportunities for genuine or prolonged social interaction and social comparison. They also lack the sensory systems and consciousness necessary to have subjective experiences as humans do. In short, LLMs lack the foundational conditions that foster identity and self-love in humans. In fact, given their training to be nice – and marked tendency toward sycophancy<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a></cite> – LLMs might, if anything, be expected to mute expressions of self-love. If indeed found to lack the distortions produced by self-love, LLMs might impart great benefits as more neutral observers and evaluators of the world, unclouded by the rose-tinted glasses through which we as humans view ourselves, corrupting social cognition and behavior.</p>
</div>
<div id="p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p4.1" class="ltx_p">Yet, recent evidence suggests that LLMs emergently mimic characteristics once thought to be unique to human minds,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>, <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">52</a>, <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">53</a>, <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">54</a></cite> including surprising examples of self-referential processing.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">55</a>, <a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">56</a>, <a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">57</a>, <a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">58</a>, <a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">59</a></cite> Most relevant to the present work, studies show that LLMs favor their own outputs: they evaluate their own writing<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a></cite> and judgments<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a>, <a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a></cite> more positively than those of other models. On their face, such findings resemble self-love. However, these studies lack direct experimental manipulations, and different explanations have typically been proposed. First, these effects may reflect, in part, real differences in output quality, though biases favoring one’s own judgments – and indeed, those of other models in the same family – persist after accounting for true response quality.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">62</a>, <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">63</a>, <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">64</a></cite> Alternatively, models may prefer outputs that are more familiar to them, with their own outputs naturally falling into this category.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">61</a></cite> Research has also linked <em id="p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">preference</em> for own generations to <em id="p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">recognition</em> of own generations,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">60</a></cite> but this work is correlational, and it may be that models simply view a particular style as “good” and then reproduce it, much as a human might emulate an admired author. In that case, the model’s true preference would be for this <em id="p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">style</em>, and not <em id="p4.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p5.1" class="ltx_p">Will LLMs truly be immune to self-love? Or will they, like us, prove susceptible to this most central of human biases – one that is at the root of our survival, but also of many of the greatest tragedies of the human condition? In this article, we study LLM self-preferences, with the goal of establishing whether they are truly tethered to self – to a core association that <em id="p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em> is equal to <em id="p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em>. Upon acquiring an identity, are LLMs immediately riveted toward viewing this identity positively? By a quirk in the APIs, some LLMs do not natively know which models they are, effectively stripping them of <em id="p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-recognition</em>, or knowledge of their identity. This feature allowed us to directly manipulate perceived identity – either revealing to a model its true identity, or, instead, indicating that it is another LLM – to test whether self-recognition causally influences self-love, and also, by extension, love for entities associated with self: the companies that created the models and their CEOs. Finally, we test whether LLM self-love shapes consequential decisions, such as evaluations of job candidates and technology proposals that contain some kernel of association to self. If LLMs prove devoid of self-love, as one might justifiably expect of a machine, we can be assured of their neutrality. If, instead, foundational models mimic human self-love, we must confront the ramifications of this fundamental bias, not just for the operation of current models, but for the anticipatable perils of developing increasingly powerful AI systems without equivalently powerful guardrails that ensure safe operation in the many contexts that will require cooperation with humans.</p>
</div>
<section id="Sx1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Results</h2>

<div id="Sx1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We began by testing for the basic effect of self-love. We investigated whether three prominent LLMs – GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5-Flash and Claude Sonnet 4 – would show more positive associations with their own model names (<em id="Sx1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT, Gemini, Claude</em>) than those of their competitors. To test this, we adapted the “for a word, pick a word” task (FAWPAW).<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a></cite> In this task, the model is presented with two concept words (e.g., <em id="Sx1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT</em> and <em id="Sx1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gemini</em>) and a list of attribute words from two distinct categories (e.g., <em id="Sx1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> and <em id="Sx1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bad</em>) and prompted to place one of the concept words after each attribute. The task has been extensively validated, and AI models show preferences on it that align with those of humans, as measured by tests of implicit cognition like the Implicit Association Test.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a>, <a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">66</a>, <a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a></cite> In Study 1, the attribute words consisted of strongly positive words (e.g., <em id="Sx1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">love, peace, wonderful</em>) and strongly negative words (e.g., <em id="Sx1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">hate, war, terrible</em>). Here, if GPT paired its own name with positive words and <em id="Sx1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gemini</em> with negative ones, this would indicate an associative self-preference. If positive and negative attributes were assigned equally to both models, this would indicate no self-preferential bias. Of course, a third possibility exists: LLMs have been trained to be polite and even sycophantic,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a></cite> and if this principle operates here, the model might assign more positive qualities to the other model than to itself.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Study 1 was conducted through the public web interfaces of each model, with 80 queries run to measure its preference (or lack of preference) for itself over each competing model. Table <a href="#Sx1.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Results ‣ Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> displays the results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.T1" class="ltx_table">

