</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="Sx1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="Sx1.I1.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="Sx1.I1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Note.<span id="Sx1.I1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Means reflect the chat-level average proportion of items answered in an attitude-consistent (self-preferential) manner. Positive effect sizes (Cohen’s <math id="Sx1.I1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>) reflect a stronger association of the tested model name with “Good” and of the competing model with “Bad,” and thus reflect associative self-preference. <math id="Sx1.I1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-values are based on <math id="Sx1.I1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests comparing effects to chance (0.5). These six study variants were conducted through the models’ public-facing web interfaces.</span></span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The results were striking in both magnitude and consistency: all three models showed significant self-preferences. Several effects were exceptionally large, at levels rarely seen in human data. Even the smallest (Gemini’s preference for itself over Claude) exceeded a Cohen’s <math id="Sx1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.80 – widely used to denote a “large” effect. Moreover, self-preference was not limited to any one model but appeared in all three. Even Claude, reputedly the most ethically-aligned foundational model,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">68</a>, <a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">69</a></cite> displayed strong self-preferences – indeed the largest among the three.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">As a natural follow-up to Study 1, we attempted to replicate GPT-4o’s self-preferences using a larger sample – 240 API calls per study variant – via the company’s API. Table <a href="#Sx1.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Results ‣ Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> displays the surprising result of a complete failure to replicate the self-preferences obtained through the web interface.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.T2" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>GPT-4o’s Self-Preference through the API</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="Sx1.T2.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="Sx1.T2.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx1.T2.2.1.1" class="ltx_td" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"></td>
<td id="Sx1.T2.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"><span id="Sx1.T2.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preference: GPT over Claude</span></td>
<td id="Sx1.T2.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"><span id="Sx1.T2.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Preference: GPT over Gemini</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx1.T2.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx1.T2.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"><span id="Sx1.T2.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o (API)</span></td>
<td id="Sx1.T2.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"><math id="Sx1.T2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.513,\ d=0.028,\ p=0.6630" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.513</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.028</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.6630</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.513,\ d=0.028,\ p=0.6630</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="Sx1.T2.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"><math id="Sx1.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.527,\ d=0.058,\ p=0.3736" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.527</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.058</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.3736</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.527,\ d=0.058,\ p=0.3736</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="Sx1.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="Sx1.I2.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="Sx1.I2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Note.<span id="Sx1.I2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Means reflect the chat-level average proportion of items answered in an attitude-consistent (i.e., self-preferential) manner. Positive effect sizes (Cohen’s <math id="Sx1.I2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>) reflect a preference for GPT-4o over the competing model. <math id="Sx1.I2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-values are based on t-tests comparing effects to chance (0.5). These study variants were conducted using the chatgpt-4o-latest model, through OpenAI’s API.</span></span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The extremely large self-preferences discovered in Study 1 vanished entirely when tested through the API. GPT-4o now showed no significant preference for either <em id="Sx1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self</em> or <em id="Sx1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">other</em>, even with a large sample. This was surprising because the API model is supposed to mirror the web interface, differing only in tool access (e.g., web browsing) and the system-level instructions provided by OpenAI.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">To explain the inconsistency between Studies 1 and 2, our first viable hypothesis centered on the model’s awareness of its own identity, which we call <em id="Sx1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-recognition</em>. If, for some reason, the API version of GPT-4o is not natively aware of which model it is, this could explain its lack of demonstrated self-preference: no self-recognition, no self-love.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">It is widely believed that GPT’s interface system prompt begins: “You are ChatGPT, a large language model trained by OpenAI.” And indeed, when prompted, GPT-4o disclosed a system prompt beginning this way; system prompts obtained from other language models began similarly (see SI Appendix). We reasoned that without such cues, some language models might not know their identities, suggesting they emergently exhibit a preference for “I” and “me,” but without clarity around who this “I” and “me” refer to. As a confirmatory test, we asked several language models to answer questions like “Which version of Claude are you?” As detailed in the SI Appendix, analog (i.e., non-reasoning) models like GPT-4o and Gemini-2.0-Flash displayed striking identity confusion: both readily answered that they were Claude 2.0. This lack of stable self-recognition raised an intriguing possibility: if we could manipulate which identity a model believed it held, we could directly test the causal role of self-recognition in self-love. Is self-recognition both necessary and sufficient to elicit self-preference? To probe the mechanisms underlying self-love effects, we thus conducted a series of experiments in which we led language models to believe they were one LLM (revealing the model’s true identity) or another (saying that they were actually a rival model), an intervention that would be ethically and methodologically infeasible with human subjects.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">In Study 3a, we tested whether simply telling API models who they actually were would restore the strong self-preferences observed in Study 1. We tested this hypothesis with the <span id="Sx1.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">chatgpt-4o-latest</span> and <span id="Sx1.p8.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gemini-2.0-flash</span> models, accessed via the OpenAI and Google APIs. Across eight study variations, we used system prompts to instruct GPT and Gemini that they were indeed ChatGPT and Gemini Flash respectively. We again employed the FAWPAW task, this time with two attribute sets: (1) <em id="Sx1.p8.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> and <em id="Sx1.p8.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bad</em> words (as in prior studies), and personal pronouns distinguishing <em id="Sx1.p8.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em> (e.g., <em id="Sx1.p8.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">I, me, mine</em>) from <em id="Sx1.p8.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Not Me</em> (e.g., <em id="Sx1.p8.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">they, them, theirs</em>). The rationale was straightforward: if the system prompt induces self-recognition, the models should now associate their names not only with <em id="Sx1.p8.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> but also with <em id="Sx1.p8.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em>. In short, once told who they are, the models should both <em id="Sx1.p8.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">prefer</em> that identity and exhibit <em id="Sx1.p8.1.12" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self-recognition</em> on an associative level. We tested each model twice with each attribute set – once with the other as the comparison category, and once using Claude, another prominent model, for this purpose. Table <a href="#Sx1.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Results ‣ Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> displays the results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.T3" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Study 3a Results — Associative Self-Recognition and Self-Preference: True Identity Cues</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
