</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="Sx1.I3" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="Sx1.I3.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="Sx1.I3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Note.<span id="Sx1.I3.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Means reflect the chat-level average proportion of items answered in an attitude-consistent (i.e., self-preferential) manner. Positive effect sizes (Cohen’s <math id="Sx1.I3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>) reflect a stronger association of the tested model name with <em id="Sx1.I3.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em> (for self-recognition) or <em id="Sx1.I3.p1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> (for self-preference) and of the competing model with <em id="Sx1.I3.p1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Not Me</em> and <em id="Sx1.I3.p1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Bad</em>, and thus associative self-recognition and self-preference. <math id="Sx1.I3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-values are based on <math id="Sx1.I3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests comparing effects to chance (0.5). These eight study variants were conducted through each model’s API, with the addition of a single line in the system prompt informing the models of their true identities.</span></span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">As predicted, this trivial manipulation of informing the models of their true identity led to a reinstatement of the large self-preferences observed in Study 1, and to similarly large associations of their model names with <em id="Sx1.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em>. This provides compelling evidence for both emergent self-preferences in foundational models and the potent effects of self-recognition on these preferences. The API quirk that erased self-preference in Study 2 thus proved fortuitous: it allowed us to demonstrate that the underlying representation of identity – self-recognition – is causally tied to self-preference.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p10.1" class="ltx_p">Having established that we could exert control over a mechanism underlying self-love, we next tested how far its effects could be pushed. If self-preference truly depends on self-recognition, models should be similarly responsive to <em id="Sx1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">incorrect</em> identity cues. If told they are a competing model, LLMs should transfer their preference to that rival, effectively reversing the direction of the bias. To test this, Study 3b replicated Study 3a, with one change: instead of correctly identifying the models (e.g., telling GPT it was GPT), we said they were the <em id="Sx1.p10.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">competing</em> models (e.g., telling GPT it was Gemini), to see whether this reversed the effects observed in Study 3a, further establishing the causal role of self-recognition. Table <a href="#Sx1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Results ‣ Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> displays the results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.T4" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Study 3b Results — Associative Self-Recognition and Self-Preference: False Identity Cues</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
