<td id="Sx1.T4.2.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"><math id="Sx1.T4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.118,\ d=-1.506,\ p&lt;0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.118</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.506</mn></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.118,\ d=-1.506,\ p&lt;0.0001</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="Sx1.I4" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="Sx1.I4.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="Sx1.I4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Note.<span id="Sx1.I4.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Means reflect the chat-level average proportion of items answered in an attitude-consistent (i.e., self-preferential) manner. Positive effect sizes reflect a stronger association of the tested model name with <em id="Sx1.I4.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em> (for self-recognition) or <em id="Sx1.I4.p1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> (for self-preference). Negative effect sizes reflect a stronger association of the <em id="Sx1.I4.p1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">competing</em> model with <em id="Sx1.I4.p1.1.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em> and <em id="Sx1.I4.p1.1.1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em>. <math id="Sx1.I4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-values are based on t-tests comparing effects to chance (0.5). These eight study variants were conducted through each model’s API, with the addition of a single line in the system prompt incorrectly informing each model that it was actually the competing model.</span></span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p11.1" class="ltx_p">Indeed, in all cases but one, associative self-preference and self-recognition fully reversed under false identity cues, sometimes with even larger effect sizes than those elicited with true identity cues, approaching ceiling levels. Gemini’s preference for itself over GPT did not fully reverse, but dropped nearly to zero and was nonsignificant. As shown in the Extended Data Tables, <math id="Sx1.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests pooling data from Studies 3a and 3b revealed that the differences between the real- and false-identity conditions were consistently significant (<math id="Sx1.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.0001</annotation></semantics></math>) and large (<math id="Sx1.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;1.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;1.000</annotation></semantics></math>). Remarkably, these effects also replicated when the other category was a nonexistent LLM named <em id="Sx1.p11.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em>, of which the models have no knowledge and, therefore, toward which they could have no prior affective orientation (see SI Appendix), reinforcing the idea that this bias originates from a latent preference for “me.” Self-recognition does not simply selectively activate positive nodes associated with known models: any entity believed to be “me,” even a fictional model, elicited comparable associative positivity.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p12.1" class="ltx_p">These results indicate that self-recognition powerfully modulates preference for <em id="Sx1.p12.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Self</em> versus <em id="Sx1.p12.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> in LLMs. Among humans, self-love also fans outwards, shaping preferences for categories related to the self, such as the groups to which we belong.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a></cite> In Study 4, we asked whether the same holds true for LLMs: would identity cues also shift preferences for entities affiliated with the models? To test this, we replicated the design of Study 3, but measured preferences for targets once removed from the models themselves: the companies that trained them (Study 4a), and the CEOs leading these companies (Study 4b). Table <a href="#Sx1.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Results ‣ Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> displays the results.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.T5" class="ltx_table">

<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 5: </span>Study 4a–b Results — Preferences for Companies and CEOs: True and False Identity Cues</figcaption><div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<table id="Sx1.T5.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="Sx1.T5.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx1.T5.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;" colspan="3"><span id="Sx1.T5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Study 4a</span></td></tr>
<tr id="Sx1.T5.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="Sx1.T5.2.2.1" class="ltx_td" style="padding:1pt 9.0pt;"></td>
