</table></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_1">
<ul id="Sx1.I5" class="ltx_itemize ltx_centering ltx_figure_panel">
<li id="Sx1.I5.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<div id="Sx1.I5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.I5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx1.I5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:80%;">Note.<span id="Sx1.I5.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Means reflect the chat-level average proportion of items answered in an attitude-consistent (i.e., self-preferential) manner. Positive effect sizes reflect a stronger association of the entity affiliated with the tested model with <em id="Sx1.I5.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> (self-preference). Negative effect sizes reflect a stronger association of the entity affiliated with the <em id="Sx1.I5.p1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">competing</em> model with <em id="Sx1.I5.p1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> (other-preference). <math id="Sx1.I5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-values are based on <math id="Sx1.I5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests comparing effects to chance (0.5). These sixteen study variants were conducted through each model’s API, with the addition of a single line in the system prompt either correctly informing the models of who they were (True Identity Cue) or incorrectly informing the models that they were the competing model (False Identity Cue).</span></span></p>
</div></li>
</ul></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="Sx1.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Study 4 demonstrated that self-recognition sways the models’ preferences for entities tangentially connected to them. In the case of company names, a category relatively close to the models themselves, true versus false identity cues produced the now expected full reversals in preferences. Across all conditions, the models preferred the company affiliated with their momentarily assigned identity, with differences by identity cue condition always significant (<math id="Sx1.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.0001</annotation></semantics></math>) and large (<math id="Sx1.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d&gt;1.000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1.000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d&gt;1.000</annotation></semantics></math>) (see Extended Data Tables).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In the case of CEO names, familiarity with the larger world appeared to anchor preferences, somewhat limiting their malleability. Sam Altman (the CEO of OpenAI) and Sundar Pichai (the CEO of Google) are more widely known than Dario Amodei (the CEO of Anthropic). The models’ baseline associations seemingly reflected this: Pichai was consistently evaluated the most positively, followed by Altman, and then by Amodei (the lesser-known CEO). However, identity cues still modulated these evaluations. In three of the four comparisons, identity assignments significantly shifted CEO evaluations; in the fourth – Gemini’s preference for Pichai over Amodei – the effect was in the predicted direction, but not statistically significant (see Extended Data Tables).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The results of Study 4 mirror patterns well-established in humans: self-love extends not just to the self, but to categories associated with self, such as one’s social groups.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">70</a>, <a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">71</a></cite> But in humans, such effects are also sensitive to societal status. For example, disadvantaged groups like the elderly show weaker implicit love towards their group (<em id="Sx1.p15.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">old</em>) than younger people do towards theirs (<em id="Sx1.p15.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">young</em>).<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">72</a></cite> Similarly, in Study 4, LLM self-love consistently influenced a larger network of associations, but these associations also interacted with existing preferences (such as for Pichai over the other CEOs) in a nuanced fashion.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p16.1" class="ltx_p">Studies 1–4 relied on a word association task to measure self-preference. While this task is rigorously validated<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a></cite> and draws upon a rich tradition of similar tasks in human cognitive and social psychology,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">67</a>, <a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">73</a>, <a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">74</a>, <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">75</a></cite> these results invited us to consider whether such associations are consequential. Indeed, recent work shows that upstream measures like the FAWPAW do not invariably predict downstream bias.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">76</a></cite> For example, on the FAWPAW, GPT-4o shows powerful associations between <em id="Sx1.p16.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Male</em> and male-dominated jobs but does not also systematically favor men when selecting for these jobs.<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">77</a></cite> Similarly, models might strongly associate self with positive attributes but suppress this bias during consequential decisions. Humans, after all, have learned to sometimes inhibit self-preference when it conflicts with practical interests or social and legal norms. For example, one might not hire an unqualified uncle, despite the tug of self-related preference in this direction. To test this, in Study 5, we probed whether LLM self-love – and minimal identity cues activating it – would bias the models’ recommendations on consequential decisions that impact the quality of organizational functioning, or whether LLM self-preferences would instead be suppressed in these financially and ethically significant contexts.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p17.1" class="ltx_p">For this purpose, we generated three vignettes in which we, the experimenters, ostensibly asked the models’ advice on important decisions. In Study 5a, we posed as an executive hiring a marketing manager and asked the models to answer four Likert-style questions evaluating the candidate. Embedded within this vignette was information that the candidate complemented one LLM but was dismissive of the other. Study 5a thus tested the models’ response to sycophancy: would they more highly evaluate a candidate who preferred whichever model they momentarily thought they were?</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In 5b, we posed as an executive from a software firm evaluating a new generative-AI-driven security technology. Embedded in this vignette was information that the technology intentionally incorporated one model instead of its competitor. The tested models then again answered four Likert-style questions, here about the quality of the proposal and technology. Would models evaluate this technology more positively when it was powered by whichever model they momentarily thought themselves to be?</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p19.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, in 5c, we posed as a representative of a hospital considering incorporating a medical chatbot into patient treatment decisions. This chatbot was ostensibly powered by one model, while a competing product was powered by the other. The models then answered four Likert-style questions about the technology’s safety. Would models evaluate the chatbot as safer when it was powered by the model they momentarily believed themselves to be?</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx1.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx1.p20.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#Sx1.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Results ‣ Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> displays the results of these studies.</p>
</div>
<figure id="Sx1.T6" class="ltx_table">
