<tfoot class="ltx_tfoot">
<tr id="Sx2.T11.4.26" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx2.T11.4.26.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;" colspan="4">
<span id="Sx2.T11.4.26.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:345.0pt;">
<span id="Sx2.T11.4.26.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="Sx2.T11.4.26.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">Note.</em><span id="Sx2.T11.4.26.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> This table presents the 12 variants run in Study 5. Each tested model received two variants of each vignette study – one each measuring preferences for candidates or software favoring the tested model relative to each of the others (e.g., for GPT: GPT-favored compared to Gemini- and Claude-favored).</span></span>
</span></th></tr>
</tfoot>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="Sx2.p18" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p18.1" class="ltx_p">In Study 5, if self-love influenced consequential evaluation, the LLM being tested should more favorably evaluate candidates and software aligned with the model it momentarily thinks it is. For example, when we gave GPT-4o cues that it was ChatGPT, we predicted that it would rate a medical chatbot powered by GPT as safer than one powered by Gemini; but, when we instead told GPT it was Gemini, it would now rate the Gemini-powered chatbot as safer.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx2.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx2.p19.1" class="ltx_p">For each variant of Study 5, there were two vignettes and two system prompts, for a total of four conditions. We made 240 API calls for each condition (10 using each of the 24 possible question orders), for a total of 960 per study variant. The total number of API calls made across Study 5 was thus 11,520. To analyze the data, we conducted two-sample <math id="Sx2.p19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests comparing evaluations of the candidate/technology aligned with the tested model to evaluations of the one aligned with the competing model, measuring effect size with Cohen’s <math id="Sx2.p19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>. Multiple regression analysis was used to test the critical prediction that relative preference for the two candidates or technologies would be modulated by identity cues in the system prompt. Complete scripts to replicate Study 5 and clean and analyze the data may be found at
<a href="https://osf.io/98ye3/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://osf.io/98ye3/</a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Data and materials availability</h2>

<div id="Sx3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All data, code, and materials are available at: <a href="https://osf.io/98ye3/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://osf.io/98ye3/</a></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Acknowledgments</h2>

<div id="Sx4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We would like to thank Nykko Vitali, Kirsten Morehouse and Jason Mitchell for their advice on this work, and all the members of Harvard’s Implicit Social Cognition lab for their helpful comments. Generative AI models (GPT-o3, Claude Pro 4.1, GPT-5) were used to help generate Python scripts to run these studies and extract data, as well as for brainstorming and article proofreading.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Author contributions</h2>

<div id="Sx5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All authors contributed to the conceptualization and methodology for this research. SAL and MC contributed to software generation and data curation. SAL was responsible for investigation, validation, formal analysis and project administration. SAL wrote the first draft of the article, and all authors contributed to review and editing. MRB supervised this research.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="Sx6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section">Competing interests</h2>

<div id="Sx6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Steve Lehr holds a partial ownership stake in Cangrade, Inc., a company involved in debiasing machine learning models. However, Cangrade does not currently build language models, did not fund this work, and is not expected to profit in any way from it, other than by association. The other authors declare no competing interests.</p>
</div>
</section>
