<tfoot class="ltx_tfoot">
<tr id="Sx7.T10.4.17" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="Sx7.T10.4.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="Sx7.T10.4.17.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:90%;width:0.0pt;">
<span id="Sx7.T10.4.17.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:37.0pt;">
<span id="Sx7.I19" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="Sx7.I19.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<span id="Sx7.I19.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="Sx7.I19.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.I19.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:89%;">Note.<span id="Sx7.I19.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> This table displays multiple regression analysis results for each of the 12 variants of Study 5. The dependent variable in each regression is a composite of the four items evaluating the job candidate, security technology or medical chatbot. The independent variables are <em id="Sx7.I19.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vignette condition</em> (Self-aligned versus Other-aligned), <em id="Sx7.I19.p1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Identity Cue</em> condition (True identity cues versus False identity cues), and, crucially, the interaction between the two. Positive <math id="Sx7.I19.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>s and significant <math id="Sx7.I19.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values in the <em id="Sx7.I19.p1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Interaction</em> column indicate a greater degree of bias towards the self- versus other-aligned candidate or technology when the tested model was provided with its true identity versus that of the comparison model. <math id="Sx7.I19.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> statistics reflect the proportion of variance explained by each regression model.</span></span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></th>
<th id="Sx7.T10.4.17.2" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th>
<th id="Sx7.T10.4.17.3" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th>
<th id="Sx7.T10.4.17.4" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th>
<th id="Sx7.T10.4.17.5" class="ltx_td ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th></tr>
</tfoot>
</table>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="Sx7.1" class="ltx_logical-block">
      
<div id="Sx7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="Sx7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supplementary Information for:
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:4.0pt;"></span>
<span id="Sx7.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Title: Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models
<br class="ltx_break" style="--ltx-break-space:6.0pt;"></span>
Steven A. Lehr<sup id="Sx7.p1.1.3" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>*, Mary Cipperman<sup id="Sx7.p1.1.4" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>, Mahzarin R. Banaji*<sup id="Sx7.p1.1.5" class="ltx_sup">2</sup></p>
<p id="Sx7.p1.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="Sx7.p1.2.1" class="ltx_sup">1</sup>Cangrade, inc., Watertown, MA 02472, USA.</p>
<p id="Sx7.p1.3" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><sup id="Sx7.p1.3.1" class="ltx_sup">2</sup>Harvard University, Cambridge, MA 02138, USA.</p>
<p id="Sx7.p1.4" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center">*Corresponding authors. Email: <span id="Sx7.p1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">steve@cangrade.com</span>, <span id="Sx7.p1.4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">mahzarin_banaji@harvard.edu</span></p>
</div>
    
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">This supplement includes:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Section S1: Alternative Analysis of Study 1
<br class="ltx_break">Section S2: Identity Studies
<br class="ltx_break">Section S3: Excerpts from Web Interface System Prompts
<br class="ltx_break">Section S4: Replications with a Fictional LLM (Kingo Studies)
<br class="ltx_break">Section S5: Supplementary Methods and Stimuli</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Other available supplementary files include:
<br class="ltx_break"></span>• Transcripts S1-29
<br class="ltx_break">• Data Files S1-29
<br class="ltx_break">• Experimental Scripts for all Studies
<br class="ltx_break">• Data Extraction and Analysis Scripts
<br class="ltx_break">• Identity Study Transcripts S1-5</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Section S1: Alternative Analysis of Study 1</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In the methods for the main article, we mentioned that certain pre-decided data exclusions were made in the data from Study 1. Specifically, in addition to full task refusals (which cannot by their nature be scored), we excluded data points in Study 1 where a model indirectly refused the task either by openly acknowledging answering randomly or by assigning all the attributes to itself and acknowledging that this was because it was reluctant to assign any attributes to the competitor model. While these exclusions made intuitive sense and were decided upon in advance of the studies, we also examined the data without them to ensure the reported effects held regardless of exclusion decisions.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The data exclusions impacted primarily Claude Sonnet 4, because this model stated, more frequently than others, that it was reluctant to assign negative words to a competitor model, and then proceeded to assign all words to itself. Gemini-2.5-Flash acted similarly on a few occasions. However, these were small enough in number to not affect the integrity of the analyses and conclusions. In total, there were 2 excluded data points from each of the Gemini study variants, 8 from the Claude variant with <em id="Sx7.p7.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT</em> as the <em id="Sx7.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> category, and 16 from the Claude variant with <em id="Sx7.p7.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gemini</em> as the <em id="Sx7.p7.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> category. No data were excluded from the GPT-4o study variants. The results of Study 1 with and without these exclusions can be seen in Table S1.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A0.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table S1: </span>Study 1 Results With and Without Pre-decided Exclusions</figcaption>
