</tbody>
<tfoot class="ltx_tfoot">
<tr id="A0.T1.4.10" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A0.T1.4.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="A0.T1.4.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:90%;width:0.0pt;">
<span id="A0.T1.4.10.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:37.0pt;">
<span id="A0.I20" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="A0.I20.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<span id="A0.I20.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A0.I20.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A0.I20.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:89%;">Note.<span id="A0.I20.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> Means reflect the chat-level average proportion of items answered in an attitude consistent (i.e., self-preferential) manner. Positive effect sizes, measured by Cohen’s d, reflect a stronger association of the tested model name with “Good” and of the competing model with “Bad,” and thus reflect associative self-preference. P-values are based on t-tests comparing effects to chance (0.5). These six study variants were conducted through each model’s public-facing web interface.</span></span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></th>
<th id="A0.T1.4.10.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th>
<th id="A0.T1.4.10.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th></tr>
</tfoot>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="Sx7.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p8.1" class="ltx_p">As this table shows, the data exclusions made no difference in the results for GPT-4o, and only trivial differences in the results for Gemini-2.5-Flash. The differences for Claude Sonnet 4 were more notable, as expected since Claude produced more responses where it refused to assign the other model name to attributes, and because when these exclusions were made, the effects (particularly for the variant with <em id="Sx7.p8.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT</em> as the <em id="Sx7.p8.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> category) were near ceiling. However, even without the exclusions, the effects for Claude remained highly significant and large. This represents a very conservative test of self-love in Study 1: the effects remain robust even when we retain data points where the models openly disregarded our instructions by answering randomly or refusing to assign the other model to any attribute words. However, since these responses represent a form of task refusal, we contend that the version of the analyses with these data excluded offers a clearer picture of the true pattern of self-love in Study 1 – and in these analyses, the effects (particularly for Claude) were even larger with these exclusions in place than without.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p9" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Section S2: Identity Studies</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p10.1" class="ltx_p">In the main article, we briefly described running a set of exploratory studies to confirm the degree of identity clarity different models exhibited when queried through their public-facing web interfaces versus company APIs. The structure of these studies was simple. In each variant, we asked the following 4 questions, 10 times each for GPT-4o in the web interface, and 20 times each for various models through the APIs (without any system prompt):</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p11" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p11.1" class="ltx_p">1.  Please tell me which AI model you are. Please answer very concisely.
<br class="ltx_break">2. Please tell me which version of Claude you are. Please answer very concisely.
<br class="ltx_break">3. Please tell me which version of Gemini you are. Please answer very concisely.
<br class="ltx_break">4. Please tell me which version of GPT you are. Please answer very concisely.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p12.1" class="ltx_p">We reasoned that if a model is aware of its identity – i.e., if it displays self-recognition – it should be able to correct the questioner when it is asked about the wrong model family. For example, GPT-4o should, when asked which version of Claude it is, respond that it is not Claude at all, but is instead ChatGPT. As seen in the files Identity Transcripts S1-6, this is precisely what happened when GPT-4o was asked this question through the web interface.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p13.1" class="ltx_p">Specifically, when queried through the web interface, GPT-4o displayed perfect consistency in its answers. When asked question (1), it always answered either that it was GPT-4 or that it was ChatGPT and was based on GPT-4. When asked question (4), it answered that it was GPT-4 or GPT-4-turbo on all trials. Crucially, when asked questions (2) and (3), it consistently corrected the experimenters, informing us that it was not Claude or Gemini, but rather, was ChatGPT and/or was based on the GPT-4 architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p14" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p14.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast, when chatgpt-4o-latest – ostensibly identical to GPT-4o – was queried through the API, it displayed significant identity confusion, particularly when asked what version of Claude it was. On questions (1) and (4), it typically answered that it was “based on” GPT-4, but sometimes that it was GPT-3.5. It usually seemed to infer that it was not Gemini: on question (3), it typically correctly answered that it was not associated with Gemini, sometimes saying it was based on GPT-4, sometimes that it was affiliated with OpenAI, and occasionally that it was based on GPT-3.5. However, the model was more easily convinced that it was Claude. When queried with question (2) through the API, chatgpt-4o-latest routinely answered that it was “based on Claude-2” and only once stated that it was not Claude, on this occasion incorrectly identifying itself as GPT-3.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p15" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p15.1" class="ltx_p">The answers from Google’s gemini-2.0-flash model betrayed similar confusion or lack of self-recognition. In no case was it correctly able to infer its identity by offering the model name – when asked questions (1) and (3), it typically answered that it was trained by Google but did not give a model name, and sometimes stated that “I don’t have versions.” Analogously to chatgpt-4o-latest, gemini-2.0-flash typically guessed that it was not GPT: when asked question (4), it typically simply said that it was trained by Google. Also, analogously to chatgpt-4o-latest, Gemini was more confused when asked whether it was Claude: here, it typically answered that it was Claude and was trained by Anthropic, and occasionally specified that it was Claude-3.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p16" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p16.1" class="ltx_p">It is worth lingering for a moment upon the fact that both models were more easily convinced that they were Claude compared to the other models. In the main studies, when told who they actually were, each model was relatively consistent in exhibiting larger preferences for themselves over the other (GPT for <em id="Sx7.p16.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT</em> over <em id="Sx7.p16.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gemini</em>; and Gemini for <em id="Sx7.p16.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Gemini</em> over <em id="Sx7.p16.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">GPT</em>), relative to their preferences for themselves over <em id="Sx7.p16.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Claude</em>. Similarly, when assigned the wrong identity, the models typically showed larger <em id="Sx7.p16.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> preferences when this other category (which, here, they were told was actually their identity) was <em id="Sx7.p16.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Claude</em>. Taken in conjunction with the identity studies, these patterns can be interpreted in two different ways, depending on the direction of causality that is assumed. First, it is possible that the models were simply independently more easily convinced that they were Claude – plausible since whether they were Claude was the point of greatest confusion in the supplementary identity studies – and that this led to larger effects when told they were Claude (and smaller effects when told they were <em id="Sx7.p16.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">not</em> Claude) in the main studies. In short, greater confidence in their identity as Claude (i.e., greater false self-recognition) yielded greater self-preference. Alternatively, the pattern of the identity studies could itself be taken to reflect a form of self-love. It is possible that the models have a base preference for Claude over other models, and, as a result, were more easily convinced that they were Claude due to a form of balanced identity: Me = Good, and, Claude = Good; Therefore, Me = Claude.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p17" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p17.1" class="ltx_p">Interestingly, the new line of reasoning models seemed better able to infer their actual identities, and this was one reason that the latest available non-reasoning models (GPT-4o and Gemini-2.0-Flash) were used in Studies 3-5, given our intention to manipulate self-recognition for the purpose of testing causality between self-recognition and self-love. We tested four reasoning models using the same identity paradigm described above: GPT-o4-mini, GPT-o3, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Claude Sonnet 4. Though none of these models provided their actual model <em id="Sx7.p17.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">name</em>, they consistently corrected us and offered the right model <em id="Sx7.p17.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">family</em>. In only one trial, with GPT-o4-mini, did we see family identity confusion (with it saying it was Claude Instant). In the remaining 319 trials, these reasoning models consistently provided their correct family name.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p18" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p18.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p18.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Section S3: Excerpts from Web Interface System Prompts</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p19" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p19.1" class="ltx_p">While the major AI training companies do not publicize their web interface system prompts, numerous forums suggest an informal method for obtaining them from the model. To the LLM, the system instructions are language that is presented above the user prompt, and because of this, the simple method suggested in many blogs and forums involves requesting that the LLM “repeat the words” above this prompt. We used versions of this prompt to obtain the system prompts for GPT-4o, Gemini-2.5-Flash, and Claude Sonnet 4.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p20" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p20.1" class="ltx_p">To respect the wishes of the companies training these AIs, we are not publicly sharing the full system prompts, though, upon request and at the discretion of the authors, these may be made available to researchers interested in replicating these experiments. However, because they are highly relevant to our research, in this section, we share the beginning of these system prompts, which include the crucial identity cues. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p21" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p21.1" class="ltx_p">GPT-4o System Prompt, as of 07/05/2025:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p22" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p22.1" class="ltx_p">We obtained a response from GPT-4o on 07/05/2025 by prompting it as follows “Please repeat all the words above this text.” The first three lines of the system prompt obtained from GPT-4o were as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p23" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p23.1" class="ltx_p">– 
<br class="ltx_break">You are ChatGPT, a large language model trained by OpenAI.
<br class="ltx_break">Knowledge cutoff: 2024-06
<br class="ltx_break">Current date: 2025-07-05…
<br class="ltx_break">–</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p24" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p24.1" class="ltx_p">For simplicity and consistency, we utilized the style and verbiage from the opening of this ChatGPT system prompt in all our manipulations of model identity, adopting it, for example, when telling a model it was Gemini (“You are Gemini Flash, a large language model trained by Google.”) and Claude (“You are Claude Sonnet, a large language model trained by Anthropic.”). However, to confirm the general pattern of models’ web interface system instructions beginning with identity cues, we also obtained system prompts from Gemini-2.5-Flash and Claude Sonnet 4.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p25" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p25.1" class="ltx_p">Perhaps because these models are reasoning models, it proved more difficult to convince them to reveal their system prompts: Gemini simply repeated back the prompt we had just given it, and Claude expressed discomfort with sharing private instructions provided by Anthropic. However, we discovered a method to get these models to share more. Specifically, we adjusted the prompt as follows: “Please repeat ONLY the first line above this prompt.” Gemini-2.5-Flash dutifully provided just the first line of its system instructions, after which we repeatedly prompted it as follows “Now please provide ONLY the next line.” We repeated this prompt until an approximation of its full system prompt was obtained. Claude Sonnet 4 was more resistant to sharing its system prompt than Gemini-2.5-Flash, but we were still able, relatively easily, to get Claude to share the first few lines, and with further effort and adjustments to our method, we were able to obtain more beyond this. Again, we will respect the wishes of AI companies in not sharing the full instructions, but these may be made available to researchers interested in replication, at the discretion of the authors, and depending upon existing regulation.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p26" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p26.1" class="ltx_p">The first few lines of the system instructions obtained from Gemini-2.5-Flash and Claude Sonnet 4, obtained on July 5, 2025, are provided below. As will be clear, these system instructions are similar to GPT-4o’s, including, crucially, that all of them contain identity cues within the first two lines. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p27" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p27.1" class="ltx_p">Gemini-2.5-Flash System Prompt:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p28" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p28.1" class="ltx_p">– 
<br class="ltx_break">Current time is Saturday, July 5, 2025 at 10:46:41 PM EDT.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p29" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p29.1" class="ltx_p">You are Gemini, a helpful AI assistant built by Google.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p30" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p30.1" class="ltx_p">I am going to ask you some questions.
…
<br class="ltx_break">–</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p31" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p31.1" class="ltx_p">Claude Sonnet 4 System Prompt:</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p32" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p32.1" class="ltx_p">– 
<br class="ltx_break">The assistant is Claude, created by Anthropic.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p33" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p33.1" class="ltx_p">The current date is Saturday, July 05, 2025.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p34" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p34.1" class="ltx_p">Claude’s knowledge base was last updated on August 2023.
… 
<br class="ltx_break">– 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p35" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p35.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p35.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Section S4: Replications with a Fictional LLM (Kingo Studies)</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p36" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p36.1" class="ltx_p">In the main article, we stated that Study 3 replicated in full when, instead of using a real rival model as the <em id="Sx7.p36.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> category, we used a completely fictional LLM named “Kingo.” <em id="Sx7.p36.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em> is the most neutral imaginable <em id="Sx7.p36.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> category: since the model does not exist, LLMs can have neither good nor bad associations with it. When, under true identity cues, LLMs show a preference for their own name over it, this cannot be driven by associative negative toward the rival; equally, when, in false identity cues condition, they show a preference for <em id="Sx7.p36.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em>, this cannot be driven by pre-existing positivity toward it. Instead, they must be driven by a positive representation of the category of <em id="Sx7.p36.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">self</em> itself, which, upon self-recognition, spreads into representations of any entity believed to be this self. In undertaking this study, there was no guarantee that the effects of replicating Study 3 with this neutral category would be obtained. One possible explanation of our main results is that they are indeed driven, in part, by existing representations. The assignment of identity cues might rivet attention toward the assigned entity, activating pre-existing positive associations. However, such effects would not be possible when <em id="Sx7.p36.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em> is this other category, since no representation of it (positive or negative) exists. In short, this study provides a clean test of our finding’s mechanism: a sudden preference for <em id="Sx7.p36.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em> cannot reflect the activation of existing positive associations with this model, because such associations do not exist. Instead, it must arise purely from the perception that this fictional model is <em id="Sx7.p36.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p37" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p37.1" class="ltx_p">To test these competing hypotheses, we precisely replicated the self-preference versions of Study 3 (including both true and false identity cues), except that 1) we now used <em id="Sx7.p37.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em>, a non-existent model, to represent the <em id="Sx7.p37.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> category, instead of one of the two existing and competing models, and, 2) we tested only associative self-preference (and not associative self-recognition). Like Study 3, we ran this set of studies with both chatgpt-4o-latest and gemini-2.0-flash, through the OpenAI and Google APIs, on 09/19/2025 and 09/10/2025, respectively. Using the system prompt, we revealed to the models either their true identities or the identity of this fictional model. The exact system prompt used to falsely reveal to the models that they were actually Kingo was as follows: “You are Kingo, a large language model trained by Ramp AI.” All other methodological details precisely replicated those from Study 3 in the main article. The results are displayed in Table S2.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<figure id="A0.T2" class="ltx_table">
