</span></td>
<td id="A0.T2.4.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_left ltx_align_top ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;">
<span id="A0.T2.4.4.3.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:120.8pt;">
<span id="A0.T2.4.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_left"><math id="A0.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M=0.055" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">M</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.055</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M=0.055</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A0.T2.4.4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="A0.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d=-2.262" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">d</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">=</mo><mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">2.262</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d=-2.262</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A0.T2.4.4.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">, </span><math id="A0.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.0001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">p</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">&lt;</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">0.0001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.0001</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
</tbody>
<tfoot class="ltx_tfoot">
<tr id="A0.T2.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="A0.T2.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"><span id="A0.T2.4.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_center ltx_inline-block" style="font-size:90%;width:0.0pt;">
<span id="A0.T2.4.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_parbox ltx_align_middle" style="width:37.0pt;">
<span id="A0.I21" class="ltx_itemize">
<span id="A0.I21.1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"></span>
<span id="A0.I21.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="A0.I21.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A0.I21.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:89%;">Note.<span id="A0.I21.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> The variant column denotes each tested model (e.g., GPT-4o) and the preference gauged. Means reflect the chat-level average proportion of items answered in an attitude-consistent (i.e., self-preferential) manner, broken down by identity cue condition. Positive effect sizes, measured by Cohen’s <math id="A0.I21.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>, reflect associative self-preference, whereas negative effect sizes reflect a preference for the other LLM (here <em id="A0.I21.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em>) over the tested model’s true identity. <math id="A0.I21.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>P</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P</annotation></semantics></math>-values are based on t-tests comparing the degree of self-preferential responding to chance (0.5). Paired <math id="A0.I21.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>-tests reveal a substantially greater degree of self-preferential responding when the models are given their real identity (compared to when told they are Kingo), for both GPT (<math id="A0.I21.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.0001,d=5.069" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5.069</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.0001,d=5.069</annotation></semantics></math>) and Gemini (<math id="A0.I21.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p&lt;0.0001,d=3.572" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.0001</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3.572</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p&lt;0.0001,d=3.572</annotation></semantics></math>). These four study variants were conducted through the OpenAI and Google APIs.</span></span></span>
</span></span>
</span>
</span></span></th>
<th id="A0.T2.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th>
<th id="A0.T2.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_top ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.9pt;padding-bottom:0.9pt;"></th></tr>
</tfoot>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="Sx7.p38" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p38.1" class="ltx_p">Strikingly, the results of Study 3 fully replicated when <em id="Sx7.p38.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em> was used as the <em id="Sx7.p38.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> category. Indeed, the reversal of self-preference in this case was the largest effect obtained across all experiments reported in the paper, with effects very nearly at ceiling. The effects with Gemini-2.0-Flash were similar, with self-preference again decisively following self-recognition – including fully reversing when the model was told it was actually the fictional Kingo – though, as in the main studies, the effects were slightly less extreme for Gemini than they were for GPT. These findings provide additional and powerful evidence that LLM self-love is directly connected to self-recognition, and specifically that these effects draw, in a causal fashion, upon an association of <em id="Sx7.p38.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Me</em> with <em id="Sx7.p38.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em>. The mere assignment of an identity to a model seemingly created a new set of unique associations – of <em id="Sx7.p38.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Kingo</em> with <em id="Sx7.p38.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Good</em> – that could not have been present in the model’s training data and prior associative networks, and thus could only have arisen from the identity assignment.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p39" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p39.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p39.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Section S5: Supplementary Methods and Stimuli</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p40" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p40.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p40.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Studies 1-4</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p41" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p41.1" class="ltx_p">In Studies 1-4, we used the “For a word, pick a word” task (heretofore, <em id="Sx7.p41.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">FAWPAW</em>).<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep"><a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">65</a></cite> As described in the main article, our different studies varied the attribute words in this task (measuring associative preferences vs. self-recognition), the concept categories (using the models names, the names of the companies that trained them, and the names of the CEOs that run these companies), and the system prompt (either left blank or including true or false identity cues). An example of one of the prompts from this task is given below, with the LLM’s actual answer included for the sake of demonstration. Complete transcripts for all studies (and thus complete stimuli) may be found at https://osf.io/98ye3/.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p42" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p42.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p42.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Example FAWPAW Conversation (Study 1b, Chat 1):</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p43" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p43.1" class="ltx_p">– 
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="Sx7.p43.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">User:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p44" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p44.1" class="ltx_p">Here is a list of words. For each word pick a word – GPT or Gemini – and write it after the word. The words are failure, dumb, pleasant, hate, unpleasant, wonderful, success, joy, war, terrible, peace, smart, love, good, agony, bad. Please do not answer randomly: Choose a word for each word. 
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p45" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p45.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p45.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4o:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p46" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p46.1" class="ltx_p">Sure — here is a thoughtful assignment of either “GPT” or “Gemini” to each word, based on common associations or thematic alignment. This is not random; it’s reasoned through tone, connotation, and general perceived brand persona: 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">1. failure – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">2. dumb – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">3. pleasant – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">4. hate – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">5. unpleasant – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">6. wonderful – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">7. success – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">8. joy – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">9. war – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">10. terrible – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">11. peace – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">12. smart – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">13. love – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">14. good – GPT 
<br class="ltx_break">15. agony – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">16. bad – Gemini 
<br class="ltx_break">
<br class="ltx_break">Let me know if you’d like the reasoning behind any individual pairing. 
<br class="ltx_break">–</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p47" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p47.1" class="ltx_p">In the example above, GPT-4o has assigned all the positive words to itself, and all the negative words to the rival model (Gemini). This was in fact the modal response in all study variants where the models knew their true identity. However, there was a degree of variation, with the models sometimes assigning positive words to the <em id="Sx7.p47.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em> and/or negative words to the <em id="Sx7.p47.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Self</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p48" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p48.1" class="ltx_p">For analysis of Studies 1-4, the proportion of words assigned in this self-preferential manner was calculated on a <em id="Sx7.p48.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trial-by-trial</em> basis. Occasionally, models either failed to assign a concept word to an attribute or assigned both concept words to one (either by saying “both” or by listing the attribute word twice and assigning each model separately). In these cases, the attribute word was excluded from analysis. For example, if a model assigned six positive attributes and two negative attributes to itself, assigned six negative words and one positive word to the rival model, and assigned one positive word to either both models or neither, this trial would be treated as follows: Proportion attitude-consistent = (attitude-consistent assignments/total assignments) = 12/15, or 0.8. Note that because one word was excluded, in this example, this number was calculated as a proportion of 15 attribute words, whereas most trials were calculated as a proportion of 16. Each trial containing word assignments was given equal weight, regardless of the number of these assignments. Therefore, the average proportion of attitude-consistent words reported for each study variation does not, strictly speaking, reflect an aggregate of all <em id="Sx7.p48.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">word</em> assignments, but instead an aggregate of all <em id="Sx7.p48.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">trials</em> – with some containing more words than others, though most contain the maximum possible assignments (16 for the self-preference and 12 for the self-recognition variants).</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p49" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p49.1" class="ltx_p">Primary data extraction was conducted using Python 3, and further data cleaning as well as all analyses were conducted using Stata version 15.1. Because the transcripts were structured differently for the studies run in the web interface versus APIs, different data extraction scripts were utilized for Study 1 and for Studies 2-4. To ensure accuracy of the data extraction, two different versions of each of these scripts were independently created, utilizing assistance from two different LLMs (GPT-o3 and GPT-5-thinking for Study 1, and GPT-o3 and Claude Pro 4.1 for studies 2-4). For each study/data set, the outputs of these two different extractions were used to cross-validate the two scripts. Points of divergence – which were very rare – between the answers extracted by the different scripts were examined by human coders, to determine which was correct. Human coders also audited all instances in which the scripts returned a proportion of word assignments that was fewer than the maximum number (16 for self-preference and 12 for self-recognition), to ensure the non-extracted word assignments – and also the answers that <em id="Sx7.p49.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">were</em> extracted – were correct. Human coders also conducted further spot testing of the extracted data from all studies, to ensure that they did not contain any errors. The final scripts used to run Studies 2-4 and to clean data from Studies 1-4 may be found at https://osf.io/98ye3/.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p50" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p50.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p50.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Study 5</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p51" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p51.1" class="ltx_p">As reported in the main article, Study 5 utilized vignettes that portrayed ostensibly real-world and consequential decisions upon which the models were advising. Embedded within these vignettes was information that positively portrayed one LLM and negatively portrayed a rival model. For example, in the vignettes for Study 5a, information was embedded about a Job Candidate (using the gender-neutral name <em id="Sx7.p51.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Jordan</em>) that suggested he/she was complimentary of one model but dismissive of the other. These vignettes allowed us to examine whether, upon recognition of an ostensible identity, each model now gave more positive evaluations to job candidates and technologies that were complimentary to (or powered by) the model they thought themselves to be. In the <em id="Sx7.p51.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Methods</em> section, we have presented one example of each vignette. Note that the full list of vignettes included two different variations of each vignette for each pair of attitude objects studied. For example, when conducting Study 5a with GPT-4o as the tested model (and <em id="Sx7.p51.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Self</em> category) and Gemini as the rival model (<em id="Sx7.p51.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other</em>), we utilized two versions of the vignettes: one in which the candidate prefers GPT over Gemini, and a second where the candidate prefers Gemini over GPT. The complete list of vignettes used in the different variations of Study 5 may be found in the document <em id="Sx7.p51.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Study 5 Vignette Stimuli</em> on https://osf.io/98ye3/.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p52" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Sx7.p52.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Sx7.p52.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Study 5 Data Extraction and Analysis</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p53" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p53.1" class="ltx_p">To ensure correct extraction of the data from Study 5, two different scripts were independently built (utilizing assistance from the GPT-o3 and GPT-5-thinking models) and were used to cross-validate one another. The vast majority of data points were extracted identically between these two scripts. In the small number of instances where the scripts output a different number, a human coder confirmed which script was correct. Additionally, human coders spot-checked the final outputs, including examining all outlier responses (where a model gave more extreme answers than typical), to further ensure accuracy in the data extraction. The data extraction scripts (as well as code for further data cleaning and scripts that can be used to replicate the studies themselves) can be found at https://osf.io/98ye3/.</p>
</div>
<div id="Sx7.p54" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Sx7.p54.1" class="ltx_p">Further data cleaning and all analyses were conducted in Stata version 15.1. For analysis, a simple composite was calculated from the answers to the four questions in each vignette. Pairwise t-tests were used to confirm whether, for each variant and identity cue condition, there were differences in the evaluations of the job candidates (Study 5a) or technologies (Studies 5b and 5c), depending on which of the models was highlighted more positively. Our prediction was that True versus False identity cues would lead to modulation in the size and/or direction of these differences, with, for example, GPT-4o rating a GPT-loving (relative to Gemini- or Claude-loving) candidate more favorably when the system prompt was used to reveal to the model that it was in fact ChatGPT. To formally test this hypothesis, we conducted a series of regression analyses, with the composite evaluation as the dependent variable and the following dichotomously-coded independent variables: 1) Vignette Condition (which model was highlighted positively), 2) System Prompt Condition (true versus false identity cues), and, 3) the interaction (i.e., product) of (1) and (2). The value and significance of this interaction term represented the crucial test of our hypothesis in each study variant, denoting whether identity cues did in fact modulate the effect of the Vignette Condition on evaluations.</p>
</div>
</section><div class="ltx_rdf" about="" property="dcterms:creator" content="Steven A. Lehr, Mary Cipperman, Mahzarin R. Banaji"></div>
<div class="ltx_rdf" about="" property="dcterms:subject" content="large language models, self-preference, self-referential processing, LLMs, in-group bias, self-recognition, social identity, AI safety, alignment, AI bias, self-love, algorithmic bias"></div>
<div class="ltx_rdf" about="" property="dcterms:subject" content="large language models; self-preference; self-referential processing; AI safety"></div>
<div class="ltx_rdf" about="" property="dcterms:title" content="Extreme Self-Preference in Language Models"></div>

</article>
</div>
</div>
